// Poolit: placing an order on the new backend, and how the pool fee drops
// Diagram type: Sequence diagram (Eraser diagram-as-code)

title Placing an order (new backend)
autoNumber
colorMode bold
styleMode shadow
typeface clean

Student [icon: user, color: green]
Student app [icon: smartphone, color: green]
REST layer [icon: globe, color: green]
api_place_order [icon: code, color: green]
place_order [icon: key, color: green]
Database [icon: database, color: green]

// 1. Opening the app: one call
Student > Student app: open the app
Student app > REST layer: api_student_view for my hostel
REST layer > Database: run as visitor or signed in student
Database > Student app: hostels, my store with menu, current pool, pool size, my own orders only
Student app > Student: pool card shows 5 students, fee 10 rupees

// 2. Ordering
Student > Student app: add Coca-Cola and Maggi, tap Place order
Student app > REST layer: api_place_order with the login token
REST layer > api_place_order: run as authenticated, caller id comes from the token

api_place_order > api_place_order: check name, items and quantities (1 to 100, at most 50 lines)
api_place_order > Database: lock the pool row so two orders cannot race

alt [label: student already has 3 orders in this pool] {
  api_place_order > Student app: 409 order limit reached
}
else [label: fewer than 3] {
  api_place_order > place_order: hand over the checked order and the student id
  place_order > Database: pool must be open and not past its timer
  place_order > Database: take stock for each item, only if enough is left
  place_order > Database: freeze item name and price, store the subtotal in paise
  place_order > Database: insert the order tied to the student account
  alt [label: not enough stock, wrong store, or pool closed] {
    place_order > Student app: 409 or 400, whole order rolled back, no stock lost
  }
  else [label: accepted] {
    place_order > api_place_order: the saved order
    api_place_order > Student app: 200 order as JSON
  }
}

// 3. Refresh
Student app > REST layer: api_student_view (refresh right after ordering)
Database > Student app: pool now 6 students, fee 5 rupees
Student app > Student: Track order screen, fee dropped for everyone in the pool

// 4. Later: the pool closes by itself
Database > Database: pg_cron runs every minute, closes expired pools, locks each order's fee, opens the next pool
Student app > REST layer: next refresh (every 15 s)
Database > Student app: pool dispatched, order status changes
